==California attorney general (2017–2021)==
[[File:Xavier Becerra official portrait.jpg|thumb|Official portrait, 2017]]
On January 24, 2017, upon appointment by California governor [[Jerry Brown]], Becerra took office and became the first Latino to serve as California's attorney general.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.latimes.com/politics/essential/la-pol-ca-essential-politics-updates-xavier-becerra-takes-oath-of-office-is-1485281551-htmlstory.html|title=Essential Politics: Gov. Brown delivers State of the State address, Xavier Becerra is sworn in as California's new attorney general|newspaper=Los Angeles Times|language=en-US|issn=0458-3035|access-date=January 25, 2017|archive-date=May 14, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190514210449/https://www.latimes.com/politics/essential/la-pol-ca-essential-politics-updates-xavier-becerra-takes-oath-of-office-is-1485281551-htmlstory.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Becerra was elected to a full four-year term in [[2018 California Attorney General election|2018]], defeating Republican Steven Bailey with 63% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|last=Miller|first=Cheryl|date=November 7, 2018|title=Xavier Becerra Wins Four-Year Term as Attorney General|url=https://www.law.com/therecorder/2018/11/07/xavier-becerra-wins-four-year-term-as-attorney-general/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190414062620/https://www.law.com/therecorder/2018/11/07/xavier-becerra-wins-four-year-term-as-attorney-general/|archive-date=April 14, 2019|access-date=December 7, 2020|website=The Recorder|language=en}}</ref> He delivered the Democratic Spanish-language response to President Trump's 2019 [[State of the Union]] address.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Campbell|first=Alexia Fernández|date=February 5, 2019|title=California's attorney general gave a scathing Spanish-language rebuttal to Trump's speech|language=en|work=[[Vox (website)|Vox]]|url=https://www.vox.com/2019/2/5/18213248/state-of-the-union-2019-spanish-rebuttal-becerra|access-date=December 7, 2020|archive-date=November 7, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201107235801/https://www.vox.com/2019/2/5/18213248/state-of-the-union-2019-spanish-rebuttal-becerra|url-status=live}}</ref>

During his term as attorney general, Becerra filed 122 lawsuits against the first Trump administration.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.cbsnews.com/sacramento/news/trumps-final-day-in-office-marked-by-nine-lawsuits-from-california-ag/ | title=Trump's Final Day in Office Marked by Nine Lawsuits from California AG | publisher=[[CBS News]] | date=January 19, 2021 | access-date=December 23, 2023 | archive-date=December 23, 2023 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231223040343/https://www.cbsnews.com/sacramento/news/trumps-final-day-in-office-marked-by-nine-lawsuits-from-california-ag/ | url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Liptak |first=Adam |date=June 17, 2021 |title=Affordable Care Act Survives Latest Supreme Court Challenge |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/17/us/obamacare-supreme-court.html |access-date=December 23, 2023 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=January 17, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240117103207/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/17/us/obamacare-supreme-court.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== California Department of Justice reforms ===
==== Bureau of Environmental Justice ====
In 2018, Becerra created an [[environmental justice]] bureau at the California Department of Justice. It opposed the effort to expand [[San Bernardino International Airport]] due to concerns regarding [[Air pollution in the United States|air pollution]], intervened to halt a proposed waterfront cement plant in Vallejo, citing significant concerns over increased air pollution and greenhouse gas emissions, and supported the City of Arvin's oil and gas ordinance, endorsing a 300-foot buffer for homes, schools, and hospitals.<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Schwartz|first1=John|date=December 7, 2020|title=Xavier Becerra Brings Environmental Justice to Forefront|language=en-US|work=[[The New York Times]]|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/12/07/climate/xavier-becerra-environmental-justice.html|access-date=December 7, 2020|issn=0362-4331|archive-date=December 7, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201207190013/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/12/07/climate/xavier-becerra-environmental-justice.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Weisbrod |first=Katelyn |date=December 26, 2020 |title=In 2018, the California AG Created an Environmental Justice Bureau. It's Become a Trendsetter |url=https://insideclimatenews.org/news/26122020/california-attorney-general-xavier-becerra-environmental-justice-bureau/ |access-date=December 22, 2023 |website=Inside Climate News |language=en-US}}</ref>

==== Protection of endangered species in Sacramento and San Joaquin River ====
On May 11, 2020, Becerra secured a preliminary injunction in this lawsuit against the Trump Administration for unlawfully expanding water export operations in the Central Valley.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Beitsch |first=Rebecca |date=May 12, 2020 |title=Court sides with California, blocking Trump's water diversion |url=https://thehill.com/policy/energy-environment/497293-court-sides-with-california-blocking-trumps-waterway-diversion/ |access-date=December 24, 2023 |website=The Hill |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Criminal law enforcement ===
On June 4, 2019, Becerra announced the arrest of [[Naasón Joaquín García]], leader of the Mexican [[megachurch]] [[La Luz Del Mundo]] and other co-defendants. Charges included human trafficking, production of child pornography, and forcible rape of a minor. On June 8, 2022, Naasón Joaquín García was sentenced to more than 16 years in a California prison.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 8, 2022 |title=Mexican megachurch leader gets more than 16 years for abuse |url=https://apnews.com/article/california-los-angeles-religion-sexual-abuse-by-clergy-c19671505d0fe989cc6d32427320f03d |access-date=January 14, 2024 |website=AP News |language=en |archive-date=January 14, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240114192603/https://apnews.com/article/california-los-angeles-religion-sexual-abuse-by-clergy-c19671505d0fe989cc6d32427320f03d |url-status=live }}</ref> In the same year, Becerra filed charges against a number of men for their alleged involvement in a statewide organized crime ring engaged in sex trafficking, tax fraud, and money laundering. Five suspects were sentenced in March 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Childs |first=Jeremy |title=Five sentenced in statewide sex trafficking ring discovered by Thousand Oaks detectives |url=https://www.vcstar.com/story/news/courts/2022/03/28/human-trafficking-ring-busted-ventura-county-detectives-california-human-traffickers-used-backpage/7194464001/ |access-date=January 14, 2024 |website=Ventura County Star |language=en-US}}</ref>

Becerra brought fourteen felony charges against [[Center for Medical Progress]] activists for [[Planned Parenthood 2015 undercover videos controversy|secretly recording fourteen videos]], and one felony charge for conspiring to invade privacy, on March 28, 2017.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pérez-Peña |first1=Richard |date=March 30, 2017 |title=Anti-Abortion Activists Charged in Planned Parenthood Video Case |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/29/us/planned-parenthood-video-charges.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170330224303/https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/29/us/planned-parenthood-video-charges.html |archive-date=March 30, 2017 |access-date=March 31, 2017 |work=[[The New York Times]] |page=A22}}</ref> Two members of the group pleaded no contest to a felony each in 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Activists whose videos accused Planned Parenthood of selling fetal remains plead to felony |url=https://www.capradio.org/205196 |access-date=2026-06-06 |website=www.capradio.org |language=en}}</ref> In August 2018, an operation targeted the [[MS-13]] gang in the [[Central Valley (California)|Central Valley]] and beyond. The following year, multiple operations were directed against Norteño street gangs in Kings, Tulare, and Stanislaus Counties, as well as in Stockton.<ref>{{cite web |title=25 Arrested in MS-13 Gang Takedown in Fresno County |url=https://cslea.com/2018/09/25-arrested-in-ms-13-gang-takedown-in-fresno-county/ |website=California Statewide Law Enforcement Association |date=4 September 2018}}</ref> In December 2019, the Country Boy Crips in south [[Bakersfield]] were the focus of a significant operation.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://gvwire.com/2019/12/18/joint-task-force-arrests-35-country-boys-crips-gang-members/ |title=Joint Task Force Arrests 35 Country Boys Crips Gang Members |date=December 18, 2019 |website=GV Wire}}</ref>

In December 2020, Becerra was faulted by state district attorneys for not taking leadership to help stop unemployment fraud during the COVID-19 pandemic, in what was described as the "biggest taxpayer fraud in California history".<ref name="Chabria">{{Cite news |last1=Chabria |first1=Anita |last2=McGreevy |first2=Patrick |last3=Winton |first3=Richard |date=December 3, 2020 |title=Some California prosecutors say 'tsunami' of prison unemployment fraud fuels street crime |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |url=https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-12-03/california-unemployment-benefits-fraud-newsom-becerra |url-status=live |access-date=December 6, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201207033809/https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-12-03/california-unemployment-benefits-fraud-newsom-becerra |archive-date=December 7, 2020}}</ref> In January 2021, investigators said the total fraud was over $11 billion, with $19 billion in claims still under investigation.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2021-01-25/california-unemployment-fraud-11-billion-investigations |title=California officials say unemployment fraud now totals more than $11 billion |first1=Patrick |last1=McGreevy |date=January 25, 2021 |website=Los Angeles Times |access-date=January 27, 2021 |archive-date=September 18, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210918114702/https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2021-01-25/california-unemployment-fraud-11-billion-investigations |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://abc7news.com/california-edd-unemployment-fraud-ui-insurance/9596289/|title=California EDD: Unemployment fraud in state 4 times worse than first reported|website=abc7news.com|date=January 13, 2021 |access-date=February 28, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|title=California unemployment fraud during pandemic may total $2B, Bank of America says|date=December 7, 2020|url=https://ktla.com/news/california/california-unemployment-fraud-during-pandemic-may-total-2b-bank-of-america-says/|access-date=December 16, 2020|publisher=[[KTLA]]|language=en-US|archive-date=December 8, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201208115013/https://ktla.com/news/california/california-unemployment-fraud-during-pandemic-may-total-2b-bank-of-america-says/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Most of this money will likely never be recovered, prosecutors said.<ref name="Chabria" /><ref>{{Cite news|last1=McGreevy|first1=Patrick|last2=Chabria|first2=Anita|last3=Winton|first3=Richard|date=December 4, 2020|title=California may have sent $1 billion in jobless benefits to people outside the state, D.A.s warn|work=[[Los Angeles Times]]|url=https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-12-04/california-unemployment-benefits-out-of-state-district-attorney|url-status=live|access-date=December 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201205202107/https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-12-04/california-unemployment-benefits-out-of-state-district-attorney|archive-date=December 5, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-12-07/bank-of-america-estimate-2-billion-california-unemployment-fraud|title=California unemployment fraud amid COVID-19 pandemic may total $2 billion, Bank of America says|first1=Patrick|last1=McGreevy|date=December 7, 2020|website=Los Angeles Times|access-date=December 16, 2020|archive-date=December 16, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201216013532/https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-12-07/bank-of-america-estimate-2-billion-california-unemployment-fraud|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Transparency ===
In 2019, Becerra threatened legal action against reporters who had received records of California law enforcement officers who had been convicted of crimes during the past decade.<ref>{{cite web|last=Lewis|first=Robert|date=February 26, 2019|title=California Keeps a Secret List of Criminal Cops But Says You Can't Have It|url=https://www.kqed.org/news/11728957/california-keeps-a-secret-list-of-criminal-cops-but-says-you-cant-have-it|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190228070103/https://www.kqed.org/news/11728957/california-keeps-a-secret-list-of-criminal-cops-but-says-you-cant-have-it|archive-date=February 28, 2019|access-date=February 27, 2019|publisher=[[KQED Inc.|KQED]]|language=en}}</ref> The [[First Amendment Coalition]] stated about his position that it was "disheartening and ominous that the highest law enforcement officer in the state is threatening legal action over something the [[First Amendment to the United States Constitution|First Amendment]] makes clear can't give rise to criminal action".<ref>{{Cite web |date=2019-02-26 |title=California keeps a secret list of criminal cops, but says you can’t have it |url=https://www.mercurynews.com/california-keeps-a-secret-list-of-criminal-cops-but-says-you-cant-have-it/ |access-date=2026-06-06 |website=East Bay Times |language=en-US}}</ref> One of the reporters stated that Becerra's legal threat was "just completely wrong in its interpretation of the law" and "had real implications for press freedom". The reporters ultimately published a number of articles about the records, and Becerra never prosecuted them.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Lagos |first=Marisa |date=2026-05-18 |title=Xavier Becerra Says He Will Fight for California. Who Did He Fight for as AG? {{!}} KQED |url=https://www.kqed.org/news/12083992/xavier-becerra-says-he-will-fight-for-california-who-did-he-fight-for-as-ag |access-date=2026-06-06 |website=www.kqed.org |language=en}}</ref>

Becerra refused to offer independent investigation into the [[killing of Sean Monterrosa]], who had been shot in the back of the head by police. He later opposed a bill that would have required him to do so.<ref name="Intercept" /> Becerra said in a public statement that there were "significant" allegations of "destruction of evidence" in the case of Monterrosa's killing.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hernández |first=By Lauren |date=2020-07-18 |title=State to investigate Vallejo police destruction of evidence in Sean Monterrosa killing |url=https://www.sfchronicle.com/bayarea/article/Pelosi-calls-for-FBI-to-investigate-Vallejo-15416828.php |access-date=2026-06-06 |website=San Francisco Chronicle |language=en}}</ref>

Two major state newspapers have criticized Becerra for, they stated, shielding law enforcement and lacking transparency. A number of news outlets stated that a significant part of his campaign support was provided by law enforcement unions.<ref name="Intercept" /> In a move that was criticized by his own party, Becerra refused to enforce a state law that required the government to release information about police officers accused of rape and physical abuse, ultimately resisting lawsuits for at least six years and despite having been ruled against by an appeals court.<ref name=":1" />

===Death penalty===
During the [[COVID-19 pandemic|COVID-19 pandemic,]] Becerra acted to uphold a number of death penalty sentences.<ref name="Intercept" /> He stated in 2020 that he supported capital punishment with "serious reservations", while also stating that its applications were "deeply flawed" and that a number of "Americans have been wrongly sentenced to death in this country and later exonerated". He had earlier voted to repeal the death penalty in California in 2016. In 2017, he pursued the death penalty for a mass murderer. Becerra described Newsom’s 2019 decision to stop executions in the state as a "new direction in California's march toward perfecting our search for justice". The [[Los Angeles Times]] wrote in the same year that Becerra had "declined" to answer whether he supported capital punishment or not, stating only that the death penalty was part of the state law he had a duty to enforce. Two years later, his attorney general's office successfully argued to make it easier for juries to recommend death sentences, a position that Newsom argued against in court.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Egelko |first=By Bob |date=2020-11-04 |title=Attorney General Becerra defends state’s death penalty, but his personal view may be different |url=https://www.sfchronicle.com/news/article/California-AG-Becerra-defends-state-s-death-15695024.php |access-date=2026-06-06 |website=San Francisco Chronicle |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite news |last=Sarat |first=Austin |author-link=Austin Sarat |date=2026 |title=Should California elect a governor who can’t decide about the death penalty? |url=https://thehill.com/opinion/criminal-justice/5901351-california-governor-death-penalty/ |url-status=live |work=The Hill}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2019-03-22 |title=Gov. Newsom may prohibit new death sentences, setting up possible conflict with Becerra |url=https://www.latimes.com/politics/la-pol-ca-california-gavin-newsom-death-penalty-20190321-story.html |access-date=2026-06-06 |website=Los Angeles Times |language=en-US}}</ref>

An article by [[Amherst College]] professor [[Austin Sarat]] stated that Becerra had not, as attorney general, addressed the issues of "racial discrimination, the impact of geography, and the risk of false convictions" in California's death penalty cases.<ref name=":0" /> Robert Lewis, a Black man with an IQ of 70, was sentenced to death in 1991, but was later ruled ineligible due to being intellectually disabled. In 2017, Becerra unsuccessfully sought to have a court artificially inflate Lewis' IQ score in order for him to be deemed eligible for the death penalty.<ref name="Intercept">{{Cite news |last=Lacy |first=Akela |date=May 19, 2026 |title=Xavier Becerra Pushed to Inflate a Black Man's IQ to Execute Him as California AG |work=[[The Intercept]] |url=https://theintercept.com/2026/05/19/xavier-becerra-california-governor-death-penalty/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260529202432/https://theintercept.com/2026/05/19/xavier-becerra-california-governor-death-penalty/ |archive-date=May 29, 2026}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite news |last=Bazelon |first=Lara |date=2020-07-27 |title=As COVID-19 Ravages California’s Death Row, the State Attorney General Fights to Keep It Packed |url=https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2020/07/california-death-row-covid-misconduct-becerra.html |access-date=2026-06-06 |work=Slate |language=en-US |issn=1091-2339}}</ref>

While arguing in favor Lewis' death penalty eligibility in court, Becerra's office stated that his IQ score had been underestimated because Lewis' background was similar to that of "African American children [who] scored much more poorly on these tests than did the group against which the test was normed". Law professor [[Lara Bazelon]] described the argument as "alarming" and "race-based". The court ruled in favor of Lewis in a 7–0 decision.<ref name=":2" /> Two years later, the state legislature banned the practice of adjusting IQ scoring based on race in death penalty cases.<ref name="Intercept" />

=== Lawsuits against Trump administration ===
==== Defense of Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals (DACA) ====
In November 2019, Becerra led a coalition of 21 attorneys general to defend [[DACA]] against the Trump Administration's attempt to terminate it.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 12, 2019 |title=Attorney General Becerra Applauds Landmark Supreme Court Decision on DACA |url=https://oag.ca.gov/news/press-releases/attorney-general-becerra-applauds-landmark-supreme-court-decision-daca |access-date=June 2, 2026 |website=oag.ca.gov}}</ref> The Court found that the administration's actions to end DACA were unlawful.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 18, 2020 |title=Department of Homeland Security v. Regents of Univ. of Cal |url=https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/19pdf/18-587_5ifl.pdf |access-date=December 23, 2023 |website=supremecourt.gov}}</ref>

==== Defense of the Affordable Care Act (ACA) ====
Becerra led the multi-state lawsuit to preserve the ACA's protections for people with pre-existing conditions and Medicaid expansion. The Supreme Court upheld the law.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 9, 2020 |title=On Eve of Oral Arguments in the U.S. Supreme Court, Attorney General Becerra Issues Statement in Defense of the Affordable Care Act |url=https://oag.ca.gov/news/press-releases/eve-oral-arguments-us-supreme-court-attorney-general-becerra-issues-statement |access-date=December 23, 2023 |website=State of California - Department of Justice - Office of the Attorney General |language=en |archive-date=December 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231223040343/https://oag.ca.gov/news/press-releases/eve-oral-arguments-us-supreme-court-attorney-general-becerra-issues-statement |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Statement by HHS Secretary Xavier Becerra on U.S. Supreme Court Decision to Uphold the Affordable Care Act in California v. Texas |url=https://www.hiv.gov/blog/statement-hhs-secretary-xavier-becerra-us-supreme-court-decision-uphold-affordable-care-act |access-date=February 21, 2024 |website=HIV.gov |language=en |archive-date=February 21, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240221142555/https://www.hiv.gov/blog/statement-hhs-secretary-xavier-becerra-us-supreme-court-decision-uphold-affordable-care-act |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==== Additional federal litigation ====
In February 2019, Becerra, Governor Gavin Newsom, and 15 other states filed a lawsuit against the Trump administration over the president's [[National Emergency Concerning the Southern Border of the United States|declaration of a national emergency]] to fund a wall at the southern U.S. border.<ref>{{cite web|date=February 18, 2019|title=Governor Newsom, Attorney General Becerra, and 15 Partner States File Lawsuit Challenging President Trump's Contrived Declaration of National Emergency at the Southern Border|url=https://oag.ca.gov/news/press-releases/governor-newsom-attorney-general-becerra-and-15-partner-states-file-lawsuit|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190406072516/https://oag.ca.gov/news/press-releases/governor-newsom-attorney-general-becerra-and-15-partner-states-file-lawsuit|archive-date=April 6, 2019|access-date=April 6, 2019|publisher=[[California Department of Justice]]}}</ref> 

The Trump administration opened 1 million acres in California to [[fracking]] and drilling in December 2019.<ref>{{cite web|last=Seheeler|first=Andrew|date=December 12, 2019|title=Trump administration opens 1 million acres in California to fracking, drilling|url=https://www.sacbee.com/news/politics-government/capitol-alert/article238321848.html|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191216061057/https://www.sacbee.com/news/politics-government/capitol-alert/article238321848.html|archive-date=December 16, 2019|access-date=January 23, 2019|website=sacbee.com|publisher=[[The Sacramento Bee]]}}</ref> Under this policy, the [[Bureau of Land Management]] proposed new lease sales for oil and gas extraction along "California's [[Central Valley (California)|Central Valley]] and [[Central Coast (California)|Central Coast]], touching eight counties and including 400,000 acres of public land".<ref name="Canon">{{cite news|last=Canon|first=Gabrielle|date=January 17, 2020|title=California sues over Trump plan to open land to fracking|work=[[USA Today]]|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2020/01/17/california-sues-over-trump-plan-open-land-fracking/4500551002/|url-status=live|access-date=January 23, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200125012750/https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2020/01/17/california-sues-over-trump-plan-open-land-fracking/4500551002/|archive-date=January 25, 2020}}</ref> California officials and agencies, including Becerra, filed a lawsuit against the Bureau of Land Management in January 2020.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Willon|first1=Phil|date=January 17, 2020|title=California going to court to stop Trump fracking plans|work=[[Los Angeles Times]]|url=https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-01-17/california-heads-to-court-to-block-trump-efforts-new-fracking|url-status=live|access-date=January 23, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200122082511/https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-01-17/california-heads-to-court-to-block-trump-efforts-new-fracking|archive-date=January 22, 2020}}</ref><ref name="Canon" />

On December 9, 2020, Becerra's office joined 47 other states and the [[Federal Trade Commission]] in an antitrust lawsuit against [[Meta Platforms|Facebook]].<ref>{{cite web|date=December 9, 2020|title=California Joins Lawsuit Against Facebook For Predatory, Monopolistic Behavior|url=https://sanfrancisco.cbslocal.com/2020/12/09/california-joins-lawsuit-against-facebook-for-monopolistic-behavior/|access-date=December 14, 2020|publisher=[[KPIX-TV]]|language=en-US|archive-date=December 9, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201209204502/https://sanfrancisco.cbslocal.com/2020/12/09/california-joins-lawsuit-against-facebook-for-monopolistic-behavior/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last1=Feiner|first1=Lauren|last2=Rodriguez|first2=Salvador|date=December 9, 2020|title=FTC and states sue Facebook, could force it to divest Instagram and WhatsApp|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2020/12/09/ftc-and-several-states-launch-antitrust-lawsuits-against-facebook.html|access-date=December 14, 2020|publisher=[[CNBC]]|language=en|archive-date=December 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201213090012/https://www.cnbc.com/2020/12/09/ftc-and-several-states-launch-antitrust-lawsuits-against-facebook.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

===2020 U.S. Senate speculation===
In August 2020, California senator [[Kamala Harris]] was selected by presumptive Democratic presidential nominee [[Joe Biden]] as his vice presidential running mate. After Biden ultimately won the [[2020 United States presidential election|general election]], Becerra was floated as a possible replacement for Harris' senate seat, along with others such as Representative [[Karen Bass]], Representative [[Barbara Lee]], California Secretary of State [[Alex Padilla]] (who was eventually chosen), and former United States Secretary of Labor [[Hilda Solis]].<ref>{{Cite news|last=Ting|first=Eric|date=November 7, 2020|title=What happens to Kamala Harris' Senate seat now that she's vice president-elect?|url=https://www.sfgate.com/politics/article/Kamala-Harris-Senate-seat-vacant-Newsom-appoint-21-15691223.php|access-date=November 7, 2020|work=[[San Francisco Chronicle]]|language=en-US|archive-date=November 8, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108025341/https://www.sfgate.com/politics/article/Kamala-Harris-Senate-seat-vacant-Newsom-appoint-21-15691223.php|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|date=November 7, 2020|title=One of these people could be Vice President-elect Kamala Harris' successor and California's next senator|url=https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-11-07/kamala-harris-successor-california-senator|access-date=November 7, 2020|work=Los Angeles Times|language=en-US|archive-date=December 7, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201207033808/https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2020-11-07/kamala-harris-successor-california-senator|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last1=Aschbrenner|first1=Annah|last2=Morin|first2=Rebecca|title=Who will take Kamala Harris' Senate seat in California? The options are many|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/11/07/kamala-harris-who-takes-her-senate-seat-california-when-she-vp/6204059002/|access-date=November 7, 2020|work=[[USA Today]]|language=en-US|archive-date=November 8, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108025240/https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/11/07/kamala-harris-who-takes-her-senate-seat-california-when-she-vp/6204059002/|url-status=live}}</ref>

==U.S. Secretary of Health and Human Services (2021–2025)==

=== Nomination and confirmation ===
''[[The New York Times]]'' reported in early December 2020 that Biden would nominate Becerra to be [[United States Secretary of Health and Human Services|secretary of health and human services]].<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Stolberg|first1=Sheryl Gay|last2=Shear|first2=Michael D.|date=December 6, 2020|title=Biden Picks Xavier Becerra to Lead Health and Human Services|work=[[The New York Times]]|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/12/06/us/politics/xavier-becerra-hhs-health-secretary.html|url-status=live|access-date=December 6, 2020|archive-date=December 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201206225006/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/12/06/us/politics/xavier-becerra-hhs-health-secretary.html}}</ref> His nomination to lead the Health and Human Services Department drew criticism from pro-life and conservative leaders led by [[Students for Life of America]], citing his "absence of health care experience and his disregard for people of faith".<ref>{{cite news |last=Richardson |first=Valerie |date=December 21, 2020 |title=Top pro-life leaders urge Senate to reject Xavier Becerra over 'anti-life, anti-religion' bias |url=https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2020/dec/21/xavier-becerra-pick-top-pro-life-leaders-urge-sena/ |work=[[The Washington Times]] |access-date=January 5, 2021 |archive-date=January 4, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210104201358/https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2020/dec/21/xavier-becerra-pick-top-pro-life-leaders-urge-sena/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Bolton |first=Alexander |date=2021-02-26 |title=Republicans see Becerra as next target in confirmation wars |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/540623-republicans-see-becerra-as-next-target-in-confirmation-wars/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221208232815/https://thehill.com/homenews/senate/540623-republicans-see-becerra-as-next-target-in-confirmation-wars/ |archive-date=December 8, 2022 |access-date=2026-02-28 |work=The Hill |language=en-US |url-status=live }}</ref> Becerra's nomination was deadlocked by the [[United States Senate Committee on Finance|Senate Finance Committee]] on March 10, 2021.<ref>{{cite web |last1=O'Brien |first1=Jack |title=SENATE PANEL DEADLOCKS ON HHS NOMINEE BECERRA, SCHUMER MUST PUSH FOR FLOOR VOTE |url=https://www.healthleadersmedia.com/strategy/senate-panel-deadlocks-hhs-nominee-becerra-schumer-must-push-floor-vote |website=healthleaders |access-date=January 17, 2022}}</ref> One day later, on March 11, 2021, Becerra's nomination was discharged from the Finance Committee by the entire Senate, in a 51–48 vote.<ref>{{cite web |title=On the Motion to Discharge (Motion to Discharge the Nomination of Xavier Becerra to be Secretary of Health and Human Services from the Committee on Finance) |url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1171/vote_117_1_00117.htm |website=US Senate |access-date=January 17, 2022}}</ref> He was confirmed by the Senate in a 50–49 vote on March 18, 2021, with all but one Democrat present and one Republican, [[Susan Collins]], voting in favor.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Senate Confirms Xavier Becerra To Head Health And Human Services|url=https://www.npr.org/2021/03/18/978691179/senate-confirms-xavier-becerra-to-head-health-and-human-services|access-date=March 18, 2021|publisher=NPR|date=March 18, 2021|language=en|last1=Sprunt|first1=Barbara|archive-date=March 18, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210318180332/https://www.npr.org/2021/03/18/978691179/senate-confirms-xavier-becerra-to-head-health-and-human-services|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=On the Nomination (Confirmation: Xavier Becerra, of California, to be Secretary of Health and Human Services) |url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=117&session=1&vote=00125 |website=US Senate |access-date=January 17, 2022 |archive-date=June 5, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210605032857/https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=117&session=1&vote=00125 |url-status=live }}</ref> This was the narrowest vote for any of Biden's cabinet positions. On March 22, 2021, Becerra was sworn in to be the new secretary.<ref>{{cite web|url = https://www.executivegov.com/2021/03/xavier-becerra-sworn-in-as-secretary-of-health-and-human-services/|title = Xavier Becerra Sworn in as Secretary of Health and Human Services| work=Executive Gov |date = March 22, 2021|access-date = March 24, 2021|archive-date = March 22, 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210322232454/https://www.executivegov.com/2021/03/xavier-becerra-sworn-in-as-secretary-of-health-and-human-services/|url-status = live}}</ref>

=== Tenure ===
[[File:First Lady Jill Biden with Secretary Becerra and Secretary McDonough.jpg|thumb|Secretary Becerra with First Lady [[Jill Biden]] (center) and Secretary of Veterans Affairs [[Denis McDonough]] (left), June 4, 2021]]
[[File:V20220708CS-0462-Edit (52308683903).jpg|thumb|right|Becerra, Vice President [[Kamala Harris]], and [[Lisa Monaco]] watch as President [[Joe Biden]] signs an executive order protecting access to reproductive healthcare service, July 8, 2022]]
Soon after officially becoming the secretary of health and human services, Becerra released a statement praising the [[Affordable Care Act]] and encouraging people to keep enrolling in its health care program.<ref>{{cite news |last=Becerra |first=Xavier |date=March 22, 2021 |title=Statement by HHS Secretary Xavier Becerra on the Anniversary of the Affordable Care Act |url=https://www.hhs.gov/about/news/2021/03/22/statement-hhs-secretary-xavier-becerra-anniversary-affordable-care-act.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220809084303/https://www.hhs.gov/about/news/2021/03/22/statement-hhs-secretary-xavier-becerra-anniversary-affordable-care-act.html |archive-date=August 9, 2022 |accessdate=March 24, 2021 |publisher=hhs.gov}}</ref>

After the Supreme Court's 2022 ruling in ''[[Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization]]'', Becerra directed HHS to expand access to abortion-related care where it could.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Stolberg |first1=Sheryl Gay |last2=Savage |first2=Charlie |date=June 28, 2022 |title=Biden's Health Secretary: 'No Magic Bullet' for Preserving Abortion Access |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/06/28/us/politics/biden-abortion-roe.html |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241122062729/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/06/28/us/politics/biden-abortion-roe.html |archive-date=November 22, 2024 |access-date=February 21, 2024 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Under his leadership the department also expanded [[Medicaid]] and [[Children's Health Insurance Program|CHIP]] postpartum coverage for more than half the states in the nation.<ref>{{cite web |date=October 27, 2022 |title=Biden-Harris Administration Announces More than Half of All States Have Expanded Access to 12 Months of Medicaid and CHIP Postpartum Coverage |url=https://www.hhs.gov/about/news/2022/10/27/biden-harris-administration-announces-more-half-all-states-have-expanded-access-to-12-months-of-medicaid-and-chip-postpartum-coverage.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241117000652/https://www.hhs.gov/about/news/2022/10/27/biden-harris-administration-announces-more-half-all-states-have-expanded-access-to-12-months-of-medicaid-and-chip-postpartum-coverage.html |archive-date=November 17, 2024 |access-date=May 4, 2025 |website=hhs.gov}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Gill |first=Julian |date=May 5, 2025 |title=HHS Secretary Becerra touts rise in ACA coverage among Black, Latino populations in Houston visit |url=https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-texas/health/article/HHS-Secretary-Becerra-touts-health-insurance-17536934.php |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221026223826/https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-texas/health/article/HHS-Secretary-Becerra-touts-health-insurance-17536934.php |archivedate=October 26, 2022 |access-date=May 5, 2025 |website=The Houston Chronicle|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=HHS Secretary Xavier Becerra touts prescription savings in Pittsburgh visit |url=https://www.post-gazette.com/news/health/2024/07/12/hhs-secretary-becerra-prescription-drugs-pittsburgh-seniors/stories/202407120101 |access-date=May 5, 2025 |website=Pittsburgh Post-Gazette |language=en}}</ref>

==== Medicare drug price negotiations ====
For Medicare beneficiaries, Becerra allowed direct price negotiations for high-cost medications under the [[Inflation Reduction Act]]. In 2023, Becerra and HHS finalized negotiations for 10 high-cost drugs, including [[Eliquis]], [[Jardiance]], and [[Xarelto]], achieving discounts of 38% to 79%. These lower prices, effective in 2026, would have saved Medicare $6 billion in 2023 alone.<ref>{{Cite web |title=HHS Selects the First Drugs for Medicare Drug Price Negotiation |url=https://us.pagefreezer.com/en-US/wa/browse/0a7f82bb-be6e-448a-ae11-373d22c37842?find-by-timestamp=2024-01-02T03:56:59Z&url=https:%2F%2Fwww.hhs.gov%2Fabout%2Fnews%2F2023%2F08%2F29%2Fhhs-selects-the-first-drugs-for-medicare-drug-price-negotiation.html&timestamp=2024-01-02T03:43:14Z |access-date=January 19, 2025 |website=|date=August 29, 2023}}</ref> Later, Becerra announced 15 additional drugs for negotiation in 2025, with reduced prices set to take effect in 2027 for drugs that treat conditions such as cancer, diabetes, and asthma.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Office |first=ASPA Press |date=January 17, 2025 |title=HHS Announces 15 Additional Drugs Selected for Medicare Drug Price Negotiations in Continued Effort to Lower Prescription Drug Costs for Seniors |url=https://www.hhs.gov/about/news/2025/01/17/hhs-announces-15-additional-drugs-selected-medicare-drug-price-negotiations-continued-effort-lower-prescription-drug-costs-seniors.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250117235718/https://www.hhs.gov/about/news/2025/01/17/hhs-announces-15-additional-drugs-selected-medicare-drug-price-negotiations-continued-effort-lower-prescription-drug-costs-seniors.html |archive-date=January 17, 2025 |access-date=January 19, 2025 |website=HHS.gov |language=en |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== Unaccompanied migrant children ====
In his role, Becerra was responsible for the [[Administration for Children and Families]] and its [[Office of Refugee Resettlement]] (ORR) which is responsible for the care of unaccompanied minors after they cross the border. ORR typically places children in government shelters and screens adults who come forward to sponsor them,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Dreier |first=Hannah |date=2026-05-14 |title=Why the Migrant Child Crisis Is Roiling the California Governor Race |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/14/us/politics/xavier-becerra-migrant-children.html |access-date=2026-06-16 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> a system which came under stress during Becerra's tenure due to an increase in border crossings. Employees told ''[[The New York Times]]'' that Becerra urged staff to move children through the processes faster and protections were loosened as a result.<ref name=":3">{{Cite news |last=Dreier |first=Hannah |date=2023-04-17 |title=As Migrant Children Were Put to Work, U.S. Ignored Warnings |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/04/17/us/politics/migrant-child-labor-biden.html |access-date=2026-06-16 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> 

Over a two-year period, ORR could not reach about 85,000 children and reporting found that some of those children were working in jobs that are illegal for minors.<ref name=":3" /> During a series of congressional oversight hearings, federal disclosures revealed that a safety hotline managed by the Office of Refugee Resettlement for unaccompanied migrant children had failed to respond to more than 65,000 calls and safety reports between August 2023 and January 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |last=News |first=A. B. C. |title=Federal law enforcement agents are interviewing unaccompanied migrant children at shelters |url=https://abcnews.com/US/federal-law-enforcement-agents-interviewing-unaccompanied-migrant-children/story?id=124556517 |access-date=2026-09-01 |website=ABC News |language=en}}</ref>  [[Hannah Dreier]]'s series covering the events, titled "Alone and Exploited," won the 2024 [[Pulitzer Prize for Investigative Reporting]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hannah Dreier of The New York Times {{!}} The Pulitzer Prizes |url=https://www.pulitzer.org/winners/hannah-dreier-new-york-times |access-date=2026-06-16 |website=www.pulitzer.org |language=en}}</ref>

==== Additional matters ====
Becerra oversaw one of the biggest reform efforts of sub-agencies in HHS's history, including at the [[Food and Drug Administration]]<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-provides-update-proposal-unified-human-foods-program-including-new-model-office-regulatory | title=FDA Provides Update on Proposal for Unified Human Foods Program, including New Model for the Office of Regulatory Affairs | website=[[Food and Drug Administration]] | date=July 5, 2023 | access-date=January 22, 2024 | archive-date=December 22, 2023 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231222142029/https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-provides-update-proposal-unified-human-foods-program-including-new-model-office-regulatory | url-status=live }}</ref> and the [[Centers for Disease Control and Prevention]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Leo |first=Leroy |date=April 4, 2022 |editor-last=Samuel |editor-first=Maju |title=CDC announces revamp plans, hires outside official for review |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/us/cdc-announces-revamp-plans-washington-post-2022-04-04/ |access-date=December 23, 2024 |website=[[Reuters]]}}</ref>

Becerra was criticized as health and human services secretary for being absent in the public eye during the pandemic, for confusing messaging by federal public health authorities and for the subsequent loss of public trust, and for his collaborative management style while serving as secretary; his defenders said he was given an unclear role as secretary.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Diamond |first1=Dan |last2=Abutaleb |first2=Yasmeen |last3=Pager |first3=Tyler |date=January 31, 2022 |title=White House frustrations grow over health chief Becerra's handling of pandemic |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/01/31/becerra-hhs-pandemic-response-leadership/ |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240328051738/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/01/31/becerra-hhs-pandemic-response-leadership/ |archive-date=March 28, 2024 |accessdate=February 16, 2022 |newspaper=The Washington Post}}</ref> Becerra was similarly criticized following his agency's response to the [[2022 monkeypox outbreak]] amid issues with health policy communication and what was widely considered a slow response; White House officials said that Becerra sought to [[Scapegoating|scapegoat]] the states rather than take responsibility for the subpar response.<ref>{{cite web |last=Cancryn |first=Adam |date=August 12, 2022 |title=Becerra in the hot seat, again, over monkeypox response |url=https://www.politico.com/amp/news/2022/08/12/becerra-monkeypox-covid-response-00051422 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250408160935/https://www.politico.com/amp/news/2022/08/12/becerra-monkeypox-covid-response-00051422 |archive-date=April 8, 2025 |access-date=May 4, 2025 |website=[[Politico]]}}</ref>

In 2023, Becerra rejected cancer patients’ petition to use a law which allows HHS regulators to rescind exclusive patent protections for government-developed drugs making them more available to the generic market.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Cunningham-Cook |first1=Matthew |last2=Sirota |first2=David |date=March 22, 2023 |title=Biden And Becerra Let Big Pharma Profiteer Off Cancer Drug |url=https://www.levernews.com/biden-and-becerra-let-big-pharma-profiteer-off-cancer-drug/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260214090917/https://www.levernews.com/biden-and-becerra-let-big-pharma-profiteer-off-cancer-drug/ |archive-date=February 14, 2026 |access-date=May 4, 2025 |website=The Lever |language=en}}</ref>

Becerra directed an overhaul of the national organ transplant system. In March 2023, the [[Health Resources and Services Administration]] announced a modernization initiative. The law ended the sole-source role that the [[United Network for Organ Sharing]] had held over the [[Organ Procurement and Transplantation Network]] since 1986.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Carollo |first=Malena |date=2023-03-22 |title=HHS Plans Breakup of Organ Transplant Monopoly – The Markup |url=https://themarkup.org/organ-failure/2023/03/22/hhs-plans-breakup-of-organ-transplant-monopoly |access-date=2026-06-16 |website=themarkup.org |language=en}}</ref>

== 2026 California gubernatorial campaign ==
{{main|2026 California gubernatorial election}}

In the 2026 California gubernatorial election, [[Gavin Newsom]] is ineligible to run for reelection as [[Governor of California|governor]] due to term limits. In February 2024, [[Politico]] reported that Becerra was considering a run for governor, and that he or affiliated individuals had approached a political consulting firm to that effect.<ref name="Cadelago">{{cite web |last1=Cadelago |first1=Christopher |last2=Lippman |first2=Daniel |title=Xavier Becerra plots political future after Biden administration |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2024/04/23/xavier-becerra-biden-administration-future-00153737 |website=Politico |date=April 23, 2024 |publisher=Politico LLC |access-date=October 27, 2024 |archive-date=April 23, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240423103828/https://www.politico.com/news/2024/04/23/xavier-becerra-biden-administration-future-00153737 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Singh |first1=Maanvi |title=Xavier Becerra reportedly mulls cabinet exit to run for California governor |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/xavier-becerra-california-governor |website=The Guardian |date=April 10, 2024 |access-date=October 27, 2024}}</ref> On April 2, 2025, Becerra declared his candidacy for the election.<ref name="Cadelago"/><ref>npr.org 5 June 2026: [https://www.npr.org/2026/06/05/nx-s1-5845847/xavier-becerra-california-governor-hilton-steyer-newsom ''Becerra wins the top spot in November's race for California governor'']</ref> Three days after the primary election, on June 5, 2026, the [[Associated Press]] called Becerra as one of the top two candidates in the [[Elections in California#Procedure|primary election]] that will advance to the general election in November.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2026-06-05 |title=Democrat Xavier Becerra advances to general election in race for California governor |url=https://apnews.com/article/california-governor-election-primary-2026-98b2b4dcca6813c3ffeb9754bd09805d |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260607084849/https://apnews.com/article/california-governor-election-primary-2026-98b2b4dcca6813c3ffeb9754bd09805d |archive-date=2026-06-07 |access-date=2026-06-08 |work=AP News |language=en}}</ref> He will face Republican [[Steve Hilton]] in the general election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Iyer |first1=Kaanita |last2=John |first2=Arit |title=Republican Steve Hilton will advance to November election in California governor’s race, CNN projects |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/06/09/politics/steve-hilton-republican-california-governor-advances |access-date=9 June 2026 |work=CNN |date=9 June 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

In November 2025, ''[[Politico]]'' reported on a scheme in which Becerra's closest aides, including his chief of staff of 20 years and Governor [[Gavin Newsom]]'s former chief of staff, had siphoned money from his campaign accounts for years. The total amount was reported to be $225,000 over two years. Although Becerra was not accused of wrongdoing, ''Politico'' reported that he was facing scrutiny as to how he had not known about the situation until federal investigators approached him.<ref name="Politico">{{Cite web |date=November 18, 2025 |title='It raises all sorts of questions': Becerra scorched by scandal in California governor's race |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/11/18/why-dont-you-know-scandal-singes-becerra-in-california-governors-race-00655837 |access-date=November 19, 2025 |website=Politico |language=en}}</ref> Becerra had previously faced criticism for suspected campaign finance violations; in 2024, it had been reported that he was paying $10,000 a month for campaign reporting from a dormant state account, an apparent [[Hatch Act]] violation. Becerra stated that the expenditures were for account maintenance, which political experts stated to be "a very high cost to pay."<ref name="Politico" />

==Personal life==
Becerra is married to physician Carolina Reyes, and they have three daughters.<ref>{{Cite news|first=Matea|last=Gold|title=Congressman Tests His Winning Streak|work=[[Los Angeles Times]]|date=March 12, 2001|url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2001-mar-12-mn-36618-story.html|access-date=December 5, 2020|archive-date=December 7, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201207033808/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2001-mar-12-mn-36618-story.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He is a member of the [[Inter-American Dialogue]] think tank, based in Washington, D.C.<ref>{{cite web|title=Team|url=http://www.thedialogue.org/experts/?iad_experttype=75|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200202110127/https://www.thedialogue.org/experts/?iad_experttype=75|archive-date=February 2, 2020|access-date=April 11, 2017|website=TheDialogue}}</ref> Becerra is Roman Catholic.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.nbcnews.com/think/opinion/here-s-why-xavier-becerra-s-confirmation-critics-gained-no-ncna1258625 | title=Opinion &#124; Why Xavier Becerra's critics could never win by attacking his Catholicism | publisher=[[NBC News]] | date=February 24, 2021 | access-date=May 20, 2022 | archive-date=February 24, 2021 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210224010645/https://www.nbcnews.com/think/opinion/here-s-why-xavier-becerra-s-confirmation-critics-gained-no-ncna1258625 | url-status=live }}</ref>

